  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/446dddc9ec5393005b5aa9fff9d45760c6f2e80da97fd459cdd74cf4f8575f71.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x-1}+1,$ the $y$-axis and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1,$ as shown above.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{x-1}+1 \\\\ y-1 & =\\sqrt[3]{x-1} \\\\ (y-1)^{3} & =x-1 \\\\ x & =(y-1)^{3}+1 \\\\ x & ={y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}+3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+3{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}-3\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}+3\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-0)-3(\\frac{1}{3}-0)+3(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-1+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
